    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently working on a digitization project for a collection of 19th-century topographical maps. I'm running into some trouble with the Dublin Core implementation regarding creator attribution. We have several significant maps where the engraver or cartographer is completely unidentifiable from the plate or the provenance. What are the current archival best practices for recording these unidentified creators to maintain metadata integrity without making assumptions?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For unidentifiable creators, the best practice is to use the Dublin Core element `dc:creator` but leave the field deliberately blank or use a specific placeholder like \"[Unknown]\" within the metadata record. Avoid inventing or guessing the name of the creator to maintain strict metadata integrity and prevent future researchers from basing work on inaccurate information. Documenting that the creator is unknown in the scope note or a dedicated administrative field is also a helpful supplementary step.",
